  At noon the water turns to glass,
  and the sky lies down in it.
  Gulls cry over the shallows.
  A boat slides by, small as a thought.

  At night the sea speaks in a low voice.
  It repeats the same few words,
  the way a grandmother tells a story
  she has told a thousand times,
  and somehow it never sounds old.

  I stand at the edge and listen.
  The water reaches my feet,
  cold, then gone, then back again.
  I think about how little I control,
  and how much I am held.

  The sea keeps its own time.
  It keeps the moon in its pull,
  the ships in its memory,
  and every wave that breaks
  and never really leaves.

